## Why This Is Asked
Tests designing a mutable priority structure with time-based expiry and top-k queries, including updates and duplicates, under realistic constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Lazy expiry in priority queues to avoid frequent rebalancing
- O(log n) updates via delete+insert using an id map
- Tie-breaking with a unique id sequence to handle duplicates
- Top-k retrieval by temporarily extracting and reinserting items

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(id, score, expiry, seq) {
    this.id = id; this.score = score; this.expiry = expiry; this.seq = seq;
  }
}
class DynamicPriorityQueue {
  constructor() {
    this.treap = new Treap();
    this.idMap = new Map();
    this.seqCounter = 0;
  }

  insertItem(id, score, expiry) {
    this.removeItem(id);
    const node = new Node(id, score, expiry, ++this.seqCounter);
    this.treap.insert(node);
    this.idMap.set(id, node);
  }

  updateScore(id, newScore) {
    const node = this.idMap.get(id);
    if (node) {
      this.insertItem(id, newScore, node.expiry);
    }
  }

  removeItem(id) {
    const node = this.idMap.get(id);
    if (node) {
      this.treap.remove(node);
      this.idMap.delete(id);
    }
  }

  queryTopK(now, k) {
    const result = [];
    const iterator = this.treap.inOrder();
    for (const node of iterator) {
      if (result.length >= k) break;
      if (node.expiry > now) {
        result.push({id: node.id, score: node.score});
      }
    }
    return result;
  }
}
```

## Memory Trade-offs
- Additional O(n) space for id map and sequence counters
- Expired items remain in memory until explicitly removed or overwritten
- Lazy expiry reduces computational overhead at the cost of memory usage

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of order-statistics trees with duplicates and practical balanced BST design. It's a common systems programming interview topic that combines theoretical data structure knowledge with implementation considerations.

## Key Concepts
- **Augmented BST (treap)**: Each node maintains value, cnt, size, and priority fields
- **Duplicate handling**: Stored via cnt field; size stores the total count including duplicates in the subtree
- **Rank traversal**: Walks the tree, summing counts from left subtrees and comparing with target value
- **Select traversal**: Uses subtree sizes to navigate directly to the k-th element

## How Duplicates Are Stored
The cnt field at each node tracks how many times that value appears. When inserting a duplicate, we simply increment cnt rather than creating a new node. This keeps the tree balanced and efficient.

## How Select/Rank Traverse Equal Keys
- **rank(x)**: When traversing, if current node's value < x, add (left subtree size + cnt) to result and move right. Otherwise, move left. This counts all elements strictly less than x.
- **select(k)**: Navigate by comparing k with left subtree size. If k ≤ left size, go left. If k > left + cnt, subtract (left + cnt) and go right. Otherwise, we've found the node.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(v) {
    this.val = v;
    this.cnt = 1;
    this.size = 1;
    this.prio = Math.random();
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
  }
}

function update(n) {
  if (!n) return;
  n.size = n.cnt + (n.left?.size || 0) + (n.right?.size || 0);
}

function rank(root, x) {
  if (!root) return 0;
  if (x <= root.val) return rank(root.left, x);
  return (root.left?.size || 0) + root.cnt + rank(root.right, x);
}

function select(root, k) {
  if (!root) return null;
  const leftSize = root.left?.size || 0;
  if (k <= leftSize) return select(root.left, k);
  if (k > leftSize + root.cnt) return select(root.right, k - leftSize - root.cnt);
  return root.val;
}
```

